{
  "title": "Ulnar Styloid Impaction Syndrome",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/hand/6049/ulnar-styloid-impaction-syndrome",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Oct 3 2024",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Ulnar Styloid Impaction Syndrome is a common cause of ulnar-sided wrist pain secondary to impaction between the ulnar styloid tip and the triquetrum.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made by PA wrist radiographs which reveal positive ulnar variance with subchondral sclerosis of the ulnar styloid and/or triquetrum.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is a course of rest, NSAIDs and splinting. In refractory cases, operative ulnar shortening osteotomy is indicated.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "common cause of ulnar-sided wrist pain",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "more prevalent in Asians than Whites",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "more positive ulnar variance",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "pathoanatomy",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "impaction between ulnar styloid tip and triquetrum that is seen in patients with excessively long ulnar styloids or ulna positive wrists",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Associated conditions",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "radial malunion",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "congenitally short radius",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "premature radial physeal closure",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Ulnocarpal joint",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "transmits about 20% of the load through the wrist",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "increasing ulnar length by 2.5mm relative to the radius increases this load up to 50%",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "pronation and hand grasp both increase elative ulnar variance and transmission forces across the wrist",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
